paper/main.tex:26:\icmlkeywords{long context, retrieval, position effects, small language models}
paper/main.tex:32:Does adding context hurt a small language model, and do the location and topicality of that context matter? We test the frozen \modelName{} artifact on \sampleSize{} deterministic synthetic contract-clause probes spanning mean prompt lengths of \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Moving the same answer-bearing clause from the start to the end raised accuracy by \positionTwoStartEndChange{} percentage points at the shortest tier and \positionFourStartEndChange{} points at the middle tier, while keyword pruning lowered matched accuracy by \pruningChange{} points. The matched amount comparison was inconclusive: accuracy rose by \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points and then changed by \amountSharedFourEightChange{} points, with only \amountSharedEightCount{} shared cases at each tier. For this model and task, clause placement and misleading lexical overlap mattered more than context amount, and a simple retriever made the problem worse.
paper/main.tex:37:You paste a long contract into a chatbot and ask, ``What code renews the vendor agreement?'' The answer sits in one clause, but a similar buyer clause repeats the words in your question and gives a different code.
paper/main.tex:39:Long prompts make this ordinary lookup less predictable. A model may fail because the document is longer, because the answer appears in a weak position, or because nearby text resembles the question. Those explanations imply different choices: shorten the prompt, move the key clause, or filter the document. A useful evaluation must vary each property while holding the others fixed.
paper/main.tex:41:We study these choices in a synthetic, gradeable version of the contract situation. Each document contains an answer-bearing vendor clause and a look-alike clause for another company. The question uses the wording of the look-alike clause, while the answer clause expresses the same relation with different words. This construction reduces the value of literal matching, following the motivation of NoLiMa \citep{modarressi2025nolima}.
paper/main.tex:43:Our contribution is a fully paired, laptop-scale evaluation of a frozen open model. Prior work established position and length failures in larger systems \citep{liu2023lost,levy2024same}, and showed that irrelevant statements can disrupt reasoning \citep{shi2023distracted}. We instead compare topically related and unrelated filler at matched prompt lengths, positions, and questions. We also test whether a deterministic keyword filter helps the exact cases on which the unfiltered model was evaluated.
paper/main.tex:45:The experiment asks four questions:
paper/main.tex:50:    \item Does keyword pruning recover accuracy on matched full-context cases?
paper/main.tex:53:The answer is not a general context-length threshold. In this grid, the model improved when the answer appeared near the end, related filler was no safer than unrelated filler, and keyword pruning amplified the lexical trap built into the probes.
paper/main.tex:57:\citet{liu2023lost} found a U-shaped position curve in multi-document question answering and key-value retrieval, with stronger use of information near context boundaries. Our design performs a smaller clause-reordering test: the question, filler, competing clause, and answer code remain fixed while only the answer clause moves. The observed pattern is primarily recency rather than a full U-shape because the start condition is also weak.
paper/main.tex:61:\citet{shi2023distracted} added irrelevant statements to arithmetic problems and found lower reasoning accuracy. We ask a different quality question: whether filler from the same business domain is less harmful than unrelated prose when amount and question wording match. NoLiMa shows why literal needle tests can overstate retrieval ability \citep{modarressi2025nolima}; our answer clause therefore paraphrases the question while a competing clause shares its surface form. The tested model belongs to the instruction-tuned Qwen family \citep{qwen2024qwen25}.
paper/main.tex:69:Seeded code generates a company, target code, competing company and code, question, and filler for each family. The question asks for the renewal code needed to extend the target company's contract. Its wording exactly matches the competing clause, whereas the answer-bearing clause calls the same value a renewal identifier. A matched literal control reverses these phrasings while retaining the same filler.
paper/main.tex:71:For each full-context family, we insert the answer clause near the start, middle, or end. Family generation does not use target position in any random seed. The competing clause is independently assigned to an earlier or later fractional depth and remains there across all three renders. Thus a paired position contrast changes only where the answer clause appears.
paper/main.tex:74:The tokenizer, rather than a character estimate, determines prompt length. The three full-context tiers average \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Each tier balances topically related filler with unrelated sentences. Related filler consists of other companies, business records, and unrelated codes; unrelated filler describes places and events outside the contract domain. Quality pairs share the family, target position, question, and measured tier.
paper/main.tex:79:At the middle tier, a keyword filter retains sentences with at least two content words shared with the question, falling back to the highest-overlap sentences if too few qualify. It preserves original order. The comparison is restricted to the \pruningDenominator{} cases with both full and pruned outputs. We also include \closedBookCount{} closed-book questions and \literalControlDenominator{} matched literal-versus-paraphrase controls.
